        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "<p>William Harvey’s De Motu Cordis (1628) achieved a monumental scientific breakthrough in human physiology by proving that the heart acted as a mechanical pump circulating a fixed volume of blood around the body in a one-way loop. Using cold-blooded animal dissections, ligature experiments on human arms, and mathematical calculations, Harvey demolished Galen’s 1,400-year-old claim that the liver constantly manufactured new blood burned up as fuel.</p><p>Nevertheless, Harvey’s discovery had virtually zero positive impact on everyday medical treatment before 1700. Because Harvey proved Galen wrong about blood consumption, logically phlebotomy (bloodletting) made no sense—yet conservative doctors mocked Harvey as \"crack-brained\" and continued bleeding patients heavily for fevers throughout the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries.</p><p>Moreover, without powerful microscopes or knowledge of ABO blood groups, physicians could not translate circulation into safe practical therapies. Harvey had to deduce invisible \"pores\" until Marcello Malpighi observed capillaries with a microscope in 1661, and early Royal Society attempts to transfuse sheep’s blood into humans killed patients and had to be banned.</p><p>Overall, I strongly disagree with the statement regarding immediate treatment: although Harvey revolutionised anatomical science and experimental methodology, practical bedside treatments before 1700 remained unchanged until twentieth-century blood group science unlocked safe transfusions.</p>",
