        "modelAnswer": "<p>Louis Pasteur’s 1861 Germ Theory was the foundational conceptual breakthrough because it finally demolished the centuries-old theory of spontaneous generation. Using swan-neck glass flasks, Pasteur proved experimentally that airborne microorganisms caused decay and fermentation rather than rotting matter producing microbes—directly inspiring Joseph Lister in 1865 to link airborne germs to surgical wound sepsis in Britain.</p><p>However, Robert Koch’s practical laboratory methods in the 1870s and 1880s were arguably more decisive in winning over sceptical British doctors such as Henry Charlton Bastian. Whereas Pasteur initially studied sour milk, beer, and silkworms rather than human patients, Koch pioneered solid agar culture plates, aniline chemical dyes, and micro-photography to isolate the exact bacteria causing anthrax (1876), tuberculosis (1882), and cholera (1883).</p><p>Furthermore, rapid technological improvements and government research funding underpinned both men’s triumphs alongside British communicators like John Tyndall and William Roberts. High-powered Carl Zeiss glass microscope lenses prevented optical distortion, while Franco-German national rivalry after the 1870–71 Franco-Prussian War spurred state-funded laboratories in Berlin and Paris to identify diphtheria, typhoid, and plague microbes before 1900.</p><p>Overall, I only partially agree with the statement. Pasteur’s 1861 Germ Theory supplied the indispensable starting principle, yet in Britain widespread medical acceptance was only secured once Robert Koch’s staining and agar techniques proved beyond doubt which specific microbe caused each deadly human disease.</p>",
