        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "<p>Superior English naval technology and gunnery tactics played a decisive battlefield role in defeating the Armada in July–August 1588. Designed by Treasurer of the Navy John Hawkins, England’s 24 fast, low-profile \"race-built\" galleons outmanoeuvred high-sided Spanish galleons in Channel winds, while smaller four-wheeled gun carriages allowed English crews to fire rapid broadsides—culminating in the 7 August Calais fireship attack and 8 August victory at Gravelines.</p><p>However, fatal structural flaws in Philip II’s invasion strategy meant the Armada was crippled before the decisive battle was even fought. Philip’s plan required the Duke of Medina Sidonia’s 130 ships to rendezvous in the Channel with the Duke of Parma’s 27,000 troops in Flanders, yet Parma possessed no deep-water port, Dutch Justin of Nassau’s flyboats blockaded Parma’s flat-bottomed barges at Dunkirk, and slow communications made coordination impossible.</p><p>Furthermore, Spanish logistical weaknesses—worsened by Drake’s April 1587 Cadiz raid—and severe Atlantic storms turned tactical setback into total catastrophe. Because Drake had burned Spain’s seasoned oak barrel staves off Cape St Vincent in 1587, Spanish food rotted and water soured in leaky green-pine casks; Spanish cannonballs often mismatched gun calibres; and August–September gales wrecked dozens of retreating ships off Scotland and Ireland, leaving barely two-thirds of the fleet to limp home.</p><p>Overall, I agree to a substantial extent, though only alongside Philip II’s flawed strategy. English race-built galleons, fireships, and leadership under Howard, Drake, and Hawkins shattered the Spanish crescent at Gravelines, yet it was the unworkable Medina Sidonia–Parma rendezvous plan that left Spain trapped in exposed Channel waters in the first place.</p>",
