        "modelAnswer": "<p><strong>Feature 1 (Quadrants and Mariner’s Astrolabes):</strong> One feature of improved Elizabethan navigation was the wider use of quadrants, cross-staffs, and brass mariner’s astrolabes, enabling sea captains to measure the angle of the sun or Pole Star accurately to calculate latitude across open oceans.</p><p><strong>Feature 2 (Mercator Projection Sea Charts (1569)):</strong> A second feature was Gerard Mercator’s 1569 world map projection and printed sailing manuals, which plotted lines of latitude and longitude on a flat grid so navigators could steer consistent compass bearings on long Atlantic and Pacific voyages.</p>",
