        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "<p>Technological advances in shipbuilding and navigation were essential enablers that made oceanic voyages physically achievable between 1558 and 1588. Stronger, longer galleons with lower forecastles and combined square and lateen sails carried larger food stores and heavy cannon across Atlantic storms, while mariner’s astrolabes, quadrants, and Gerard Mercator’s 1569 projection maps allowed captains like Francis Drake and Martin Frobisher to navigate thousands of miles out of sight of land.</p><p>However, economic necessity and the lure of immense commercial profit provided the primary motive driving courtiers and merchants to risk capital at sea. After the 1568 Antwerp trade embargo disrupted England’s traditional European cloth market, English investors urgently sought direct routes to Asian spices, Russian furs, and Mediterranean silks—establishing the Eastland (1579) and Levant/Turkey (1581) Companies and backing John Hawkins’ transatlantic voyages.</p><p>Furthermore, intensifying religious and geopolitical rivalry with Catholic Spain transformed private commercial ventures into crown-backed strategic raids. Elizabeth and Protestant privy councillors like Walsingham and Leicester secretly financed Drake’s 1577–80 voyage to raid Philip II’s Pacific silver lifeline, yielding £140,000 from the Cacafuego—a 4,700% return for investors—while encouraging Humphrey Gilbert and Walter Raleigh to establish Protestant North American bases.</p><p>Overall, I disagree that ship design and navigation were the main reason. While galleons and Mercator charts provided the indispensable tools to cross the oceans safely, commercial ambition after the Antwerp slump and anti-Spanish strategic rivalry supplied the decisive financial and political drive behind Elizabethan exploration.</p>",
